\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{From a sign line to an exchange of two lines}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Take #{G=C_2} over the reals and let the scalar fibre be the
sign line, with orbit #{\{1,-1\}}. If the fermion fibre is also a
single sign line, conjugation acts trivially on its endomorphisms.
An equivariant [Yukawa map](fdip-011M) then has
#{Y(-u)=Y(u)}, whereas linearity gives #{Y(-u)=-Y(u)}.
Hence #{Y=0}.}

\p{Instead take #{F_\psi=\mathbb R\oplus\mathbb R_{\rm sign}}
with its Euclidean pairing. Fix #{y\in\mathbb R} and put
##{Y(\phi)(u,v)=y\phi(v,u).}
Conjugation by #{(u,v)\mapsto(u,-v)} negates the exchange, just
as replacing #{\phi} by #{-\phi} does. At #{\phi^\circ=1}
the vectors #{(1,1)} and #{(1,-1)} span eigenlines of
#{m\bbI+Y(1)} with eigenvalues #{m+y} and #{m-y}.
A negative link interchanges these lines. For #{y\ne0} it
therefore mixes the two distinct onsite eigenspaces.}
